+## Clock and offline operation
+
+Reticulum has to work with **no internet at all**: HaLow-only, LAN-only or
+WiFi-only meshes. So nothing on the box waits for the clock. `rnsd` (and
+`lxmd` / `nomadnet` when enabled) always start at boot, creating the
+Reticulum identity on the first start as usual, whether or not there is
+internet, NTP or an RTC. `ntpd` always runs, and boot never blocks on the
+network or the time.
+
+Where the time comes from:
+
+- **RTC at boot.** With the optional DS3231 fitted (below), the kernel sets
+  the clock from it while booting. `S45ntpsync` then records the source as
+  `rtc`, as long as the RTC's time can be trusted (see *Validity*).
+- **NTP.** Whenever a route to one of the servers in Settings → NTP servers
+  appears (an IPv4 default route, or a local time server on a directly
+  connected subnet — e.g. a GPS NTP box on a mesh with no uplink; give it as
+  an IP address, since a name needs DNS over a default route), and again
+  whenever the routes change, a background one-shot runs `ntpdate` against
+  them. It retries at growing intervals while the route stays up without
+  NTP, and Settings → *Restart NTP* runs it at once (e.g. after editing the
+  servers).
+  `ntpd` is stopped for the few seconds of that step and started again
+  afterwards, because two programs stepping the clock at once can apply the
+  same correction twice. The result is re-checked against the servers; on
+  success the source becomes `ntp`, and the time is written to the RTC.
+  `ntpd` keeps the clock disciplined after that.
+- **Browser.** Settings → *Sync clock to browser* sets the clock from your
+  browser's time and writes it to the RTC, **even an RTC that was never set**.
+  That is how you set up an RTC on a box with no internet. NTP takes
+  precedence: once NTP has synced, the browser button is not needed.
+
+The Settings page shows the system time, the clock source (none / RTC / NTP /
+browser), the RTC state and the NTP servers. `/etc/init.d/S45ntpsync status`
+shows the same from a shell, and `/var/log/ntp-step.log` has the NTP
+one-shot's log. The RTC is written only on a successful NTP sync, on a
+browser sync, by an hourly resync, and by the kernel's own 11-minute
+write-back while `ntpd` has the clock synced. It is never written from an
+untrusted clock.
+
+The **hourly resync** (`S45ntpsync resync`, from cron at :17) exists
+because that kernel write-back can't be relied on here: every clock step —
+the NTP one-shot's, a browser sync, `ntpd`'s own — clears the kernel's
+"synced" state until `ntpd` has re-synced, which can take a long time or
+never happen, and without `ntpq` on the box nothing can confirm it. So once
+an hour, when an NTP server is reachable, the resync queries the servers
+(`ntpdate -q`, which never touches the clock): within 0.5 s, the time is
+written to the RTC (and the source becomes `ntp` if it wasn't); further off,
+the clock is stepped first, exactly as by the one-shot. No answer, no route
+or no RTC: nothing is written.
+
+**Without a trusted clock**, Reticulum still routes, but with a caveat. Its
+announces carry a wrong (1970) timestamp. Reticulum keeps a path until the
+current time passes its receive time plus 7 days, and replaces it only with an
+announce emitted later than the stored one.
+So a node whose clock is behind the timestamps it stored keeps stale paths
+indefinitely. That happens to this box after a reboot when it ran with NTP or
+a browser-set clock, or for days on an unset one: `S82rnsd` therefore sets
+aside a path table dated more than an hour after the current clock before it
+starts `rnsd` (identity and known destinations are kept), and the box
+re-learns its paths from live announces. On an online box without an RTC
+that is usually one re-learn per boot, since `rnsd` starts before NTP sets
+the clock. Peers that
+still hold this box's earlier, later-dated announce ignore its new ones until
+that path expires on their own clock. Another RNSBox whose clock also rolled
+back sets its table aside at its own restart; any other node whose clock
+rolled back keeps the stale path until its clock is set. When the clock is set
+later, Reticulum re-learns paths and re-establishes links once. A DS3231, or
+NTP / a browser sync on every node, avoids all of this. NTP or a browser sync
+fixes the timestamps from then on. A propagation node (`lxmd`,
+or `nomadnet`'s) records with the unset date too: the messages it stores, the
+propagation peers it hears, and its record of messages already handled. When
+the clock is set all of these are re-dated rather than expired, so messages
+still get LXMF's full 30-day lifetime, peers heard meanwhile aren't dropped as
+14 days unreachable, and already-handled messages aren't accepted again. The
+rnsd update check needs a correct date for HTTPS, so it is skipped (and the
+portal says so) until the clock is set.
+
+### Optional DS3231 RTC
+
+The Nano-e has no battery-backed clock, so it boots at 1970. A DS3231 module
+(the common ZS-042 board, or a bare chip) keeps the time across power-off on
+its coin cell. It sits on a bit-banged I²C bus, `i2c5`, at address `0x68`,
+and shows up as `/dev/rtc0`.
+
+```
+  LicheeRV Nano-e                 DS3231 module
+  ───────────────                 ─────────────
+   3V3      ────────────────────  VCC
+   GND      ────────────────────  GND
+   GPIOA15  ────────────────────  SCL
+   GPIOA27  ────────────────────  SDA
+```
+
+| Nano-e pad | Function   | DS3231 |
+|------------|------------|--------|
+| `3V3`      | power      | VCC    |
+| `GND`      | ground     | GND    |
+| `GPIOA15`  | I²C5 SCL   | SCL    |
+| `GPIOA27`  | I²C5 SDA   | SDA    |
+
+- **3.3 V only.** Power the module from `3V3`, never 5 V. The module's
+  pull-ups go to its VCC, and the SG2002 pads are not 5 V tolerant.
+- **Pull-ups.** I²C needs pull-ups on SDA and SCL. ZS-042-style modules
+  have 4.7 kΩ on board. With a bare chip, fit 4.7 kΩ from each line to 3.3 V.
+- **Coin-cell charging.** The ZS-042 trickle-charges its cell through a
+  resistor and diode, which is unsafe with a non-rechargeable CR2032. Remove
+  that resistor or diode, or use an LIR2032.
+- **Why not I2C1 / I2C3?** Sipeed's pinout labels the SD1 pads as I2C1 and
+  I2C3. On the Nano-e **W** those pads carry the SDIO bus to the AIC8800
+  WiFi. `GPIOA15` / `GPIOA27` are free on both boards, so one image serves
+  both. U-Boot already muxes them to GPIO.
+- **No module fitted** is fine. The probe finds nothing, there is no
+  `rtc0`, and the box behaves exactly as without an RTC.
+
+**Validity.** A DS3231 sets its oscillator-stop flag (OSF) whenever its
+oscillator has stopped. That happens on first power-up and when the coin cell
+ran flat while the board was unpowered. Its time is then meaningless: it
+restarts at 2000-01-01. The stock 5.10 driver cleared the flag while probing
+and read that time back anyway; RNSBox's patched `rtc-ds1307` keeps the flag
+(logging `oscillator stop flag set … SET TIME!` once at boot) and refuses to
+read the time while it is set, until the RTC is next written — which clears
+it. So the kernel doesn't set the clock from such an RTC, and `hwclock -r`
+or reading `/sys/class/rtc/rtc0/time` fails with *Invalid argument*.
+`S45ntpsync` trusts the RTC at boot only if both of these hold:
+
+- the kernel actually set the clock from it (`/sys/class/rtc/rtc0/hctosys`
+  is `1`);
+- the time lies between 2020 and 2100.
+
+Otherwise `S45ntpsync` ignores the RTC's time, and the clock source stays
+*none* until the next NTP or browser sync writes the RTC. The flag lives in
+the chip, so this holds on every boot until then. The Settings page shows
+such an RTC, or one reading outside 2020–2100, as *not set*, along with the
+DS3231's temperature sensor.
+
 ## Build variants
 
